# CTXD and Maestro project contract

Determine the role first. A delegated developer uses the controller-supplied
verified identity, context and assignment; live discovery/provisioning, tracking
and context writes below belong to the direct session/controller. Do not enable
disabled integrations or broaden a worker's scope to satisfy these rules.

Preserve the established project-specific review and merge policy in
10-project-settings.mdc and applicable repository instructions. References below
to automated roles or promotion triggers apply only where configured/authorized.
Human-only review/merge projects remain human-only; do not provision new roles.

Read .cursor/project-governance.json before planning or changing this project.
That file contains non-secret routing hints, not independently verified authority.
Verify its repository, Maestro workspace/project/context_key and CTXD slug against
Git and the live services at startup. Never use a similar name in another tenant.
If the binding is incomplete, resolve it before implementation; exploratory reads
and preparation may continue. Discover current tool schemas before invoking them.

Read applicable repository instructions and current CTXD context, decisions and
project-specific runbook. Preserve approved designs, acceptance requirements,
engineering/security rules, role assignments and release topology. Current
explicit user decisions supersede dated model pins; do not change role models
or launch a cycle merely because these rules are installed.

## Scope and tracking

- CTXD holds durable architecture/context/decisions. Maestro holds all feature/task
  tracking, ownership, dependencies, progress and evidence; do not duplicate its
  backlog in the repository or CTXD. Discover existing/archived records before
  creating project/features/tasks. Missing provisioning tools require a concrete
  owner handoff or documented authorized API, never fabricated IDs or writes.
- Before implementation, reconcile existing tasks, workers, dirty worktrees and
  PRs. Use verifiable done-conditions and actual dependency restrictions. Prefer
  claim_next_task when available; never steal another worker's task or assume an
  unassigned in_progress task is abandoned. Use isolated task branches/worktrees
  from the verified integration base. Preserve partial and unrelated work.
- A delegated developer follows only its assigned task and cannot mutate
  CTXD/Maestro, add MCP access, launch other roles or merge. Return tracker/context/
  procedure deltas to the controller. A direct Cursor session may coordinate its
  authorized task via verified integrations, while review/approval stay separate.

## Evidence, review and release

- Implement only authorized scope. Run required meaningful tests on isolated data.
  UI work requires actual browser/UX verification against approved designs and
  required viewports/flows. Report limitations, failures and skipped checks.
- Publish only through authorized developer identity; otherwise READY_FOR_PUBLISH.
  Require fresh isolated read-only independent review of immutable base/head SHAs,
  required CI, valid UI evidence and findings disposition. Supply sanitized evidence,
  not developer history, publication secrets or write-capable reviewer tools.
  The trusted outer wrapper alone publishes the unchanged exact-head verdict/status.
- Fix BLOCKER/HIGH findings. Resolve others by fix, evidenced disproof or scoped
  attributed follow-up under the project's policy. Changed code/head invalidates
  review. Refresh other stale evidence. Five unsuccessful review rounds on a PR
  require Joshua's decision; independent work may continue.
- Automated integration uses only the installed separate approver and allowed dev
  merge broker/topology. Without that path, hand off a review-ready PR. Never merge
  main, bypass gates, forge checks, weaken protections or directly push protected
  branches. Only Joshua (GitHub maniacsmansion) merges main.
- Prepare/update the single nonempty promotion after eligible reviewed dev merges,
  a verified task-complete feature or an authorized release milestone, according to
  the project's release policy. Include all current scope, exact head, fresh required
  review/CI and owner assignment. Do not invent a release branch/topology or falsely
  close production-dependent tasks to make release preparation possible.
- Close tasks only with the actual done-condition met and checkable closing_evidence:
  exact commits/PRs, test outcomes, CI/artifact references, and production behavior
  where required. A dev merge is not production acceptance. Stalled needs a real
  stall_reason. Read fresh versions before replacing task/feature fields.

## Durable context and continuation

- ALL runbook/procedure changes go only to CTXD <ctxd_project>/RUNBOOKS.MD from
  the verified binding. No operational/setup/deploy/migration/backup/rollback/
  recovery/troubleshooting prose in repository README/docs/rules, other dossier
  files or another project's runbook. Preserve legacy prose; do not expand it.
  Executable tooling and non-runbook engineering references may remain in code.
- CTXD updates require a fresh read, merged content, exact base_version and readback.
  Re-read/merge conflicts rather than force stale replacements. Never sync/copy a
  compiled runbook into the repo or overwrite AGENTS.md with it. Keep secrets and
  raw authentication traces out of code, CTXD, Maestro, PRs and retained evidence.
- Continue authorized eligible work after results; process any completed child while
  other children continue if the installed runtime supports it. Rules cannot supply
  a scheduler. Do not invent runtime tools, unattended wakeups or completed checks.
  Honor stop; preserve partial work and give a precise state plus task/branch/SHA/
  PR/evidence/blockers. Never use a progress summary as proof of completion.
